WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:02.530
When it comes to deadly predators,

00:00:02.530 --> 00:00:06.350
plants generally don't come to mind.

00:00:06.350 --> 00:00:07.660
After all, they're typically

00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:10.150
at the bottom of the food chain.

00:00:10.150 --> 00:00:14.080
But the Carolinas are home
to one vicious vegetable:

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:16.380
the Venus flytrap.

00:00:16.380 --> 00:00:19.810
Using its famous trap,
it can catch prey faster

00:00:19.810 --> 00:00:21.380
than you can blink.

00:00:21.380 --> 00:00:25.293
But what happens next
inside a Venus flytrap?

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:28.410
Funny thing about Venus flytraps,

00:00:28.410 --> 00:00:31.400
they don't usually trap flies.

00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:36.330
In fact, winged insects only
make up about 5% of their diet.

00:00:36.330 --> 00:00:37.840
Clyde Sorenson: We really
ought to be calling it

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:39.660
the Carolina spider trap

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:43.720
because that's really...it's
only found in the Carolinas,

00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:46.530
and, actually, a little
piece of the Carolinas,

00:00:46.530 --> 00:00:50.308
and it mostly eats spiders and ants.

00:00:50.308 --> 00:00:52.510
Narrator: But, of course,
regardless of species,

00:00:52.510 --> 00:00:55.460
that bug is going to have a bad day.

00:00:55.460 --> 00:00:58.590
It all starts when the
victim wanders into the trap,

00:00:58.590 --> 00:01:03.110
possibly lured by the bright-red
hue or fragrant scent.

00:01:03.110 --> 00:01:05.290
Or maybe they're just unlucky.

00:01:05.290 --> 00:01:08.330
Sorenson: We think the
spiders mostly just blunder.

00:01:08.330 --> 00:01:11.490
Narrator: The trap itself
looks like an open mouth.

00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:14.180
It's made of two pads attached to a hinge.

00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:15.510
Sorenson: On each one of those pads,

00:01:15.510 --> 00:01:19.540
there are usually three
little trigger hairs

00:01:19.540 --> 00:01:22.200
in a kind of a triangle.

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:26.630
And those trigger hairs
are very, very sensitive

00:01:26.630 --> 00:01:28.500
to being disturbed.

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:30.740
Narrator: The first time a
spider knocks into a hair,

00:01:30.740 --> 00:01:32.730
it sets off an electrical signal,

00:01:32.730 --> 00:01:36.130
sort of like the electrical
currents in your brain.

00:01:36.130 --> 00:01:38.700
That signal starts the countdown.

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:42.000
If the bug escapes
within 20 to 30 seconds,

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:43.680
nothing else happens.

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:46.320
That way, the plant doesn't waste energy.

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:50.370
But if the bug brushes
against another hair, snap!

00:01:50.370 --> 00:01:53.650
In just 100 milliseconds,
about four times faster

00:01:53.650 --> 00:01:55.210
than you can blink,

00:01:55.210 --> 00:01:57.043
the trap slams shut.

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:01.790
Sorenson: Then, the trap rapidly
goes from convex to concave

00:02:03.020 --> 00:02:04.200
on each side,

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:09.100
and the long little spikes
on the rims of the pads

00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:12.030
interlock to form kind of a cage.

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:14.220
Narrator: Now, of course,
the spider isn't happy

00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:16.330
with this turn of events.

00:02:16.330 --> 00:02:18.580
So it tries to escape,

00:02:18.580 --> 00:02:21.650
which is exactly what the plant wants.

00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:24.180
The more the spider
struggles, the more it knocks

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:28.150
into the trigger hairs, the
tighter the trap closes.

00:02:28.150 --> 00:02:32.140
And after an hour or two,
the trap locks completely.

00:02:32.140 --> 00:02:35.400
Cells on the edges of
the pad secrete moisture,

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:37.130
which glues the edges together

00:02:37.130 --> 00:02:39.520
to form an airtight seal.

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:42.740
Suddenly, that trap isn't a mouth anymore;

00:02:42.740 --> 00:02:44.170
it's a stomach.

00:02:44.170 --> 00:02:47.270
Digestive juices flood into
the closed compartment,

00:02:47.270 --> 00:02:50.200
dissolving the spider's soft organs,

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:52.140
and the trap's lining sucks up

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:54.400
that nutrient-rich slushy.

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:58.138
After about a week, all
that's left is an empty husk,

00:02:58.138 --> 00:03:00.170
the spider's exoskeleton.

00:03:00.170 --> 00:03:04.510
Next, the trap reopens
and the husk tumbles out.

00:03:04.510 --> 00:03:07.430
The trap is now ready for its next meal.

00:03:07.430 --> 00:03:11.060
But bugs aren't the only
food the trap captures.

00:03:11.060 --> 00:03:13.200
Just like leaves on other plants,

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:16.000
the trap's surface
contains a green pigment

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:18.987
that lets it convert the
sun's energy into sugar

00:03:18.987 --> 00:03:21.740
through a process called photosynthesis.

00:03:21.740 --> 00:03:25.030
So then, why bother with the bugs?

00:03:25.030 --> 00:03:29.250
Well, Venus flytraps live
in acidic, waterlogged soil

00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:31.640
that doesn't have many nutrients.

00:03:31.640 --> 00:03:34.260
So, instead of slurping
up nitrogen and phosphorus

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:39.260
through its roots, it needs
to borrow some from the bugs.

00:03:39.500 --> 00:03:41.310
That explains why it shares its home

00:03:41.310 --> 00:03:43.780
with other hungry carnivorous plants,

00:03:43.780 --> 00:03:46.550
like pitcher plants and sundews,

00:03:46.550 --> 00:03:48.767
which could only mean one thing:

00:03:48.767 --> 00:03:52.433
North Carolina is not a
fun place to be a bug.

